19	                                                                               Abstract
20	                                           We present a computational framework that combines machine-learning-based
21	                                           discovery with rigorous computer-assisted numerical certification and formal ver-
22	                                           ification for strongly nonlinear partial differential equations. We demonstrate the
23	                                           approach for periodic gravity–capillary traveling water waves. Physics-informed
24	                                           neural networks are used as a search mechanism, with the wave profile, wave
25	                                           speed and, in continuation experiments, surface tension learned directly from the
26	                                           governing equations without training data. Through a staged training and refine-
27	                                           ment procedure, the neural networks achieve near-machine-precision residuals of
28	                                           the governing equations, even in strongly nonlinear regimes. A single formulation
29	                                           recovers three classical high-amplitude regimes: pure-gravity Stokes waves, pure-
30	                                           capillary Crapper waves and gravity–capillary Wilton ripples. Starting from a
